
Clam — A Perl AI Environment
============================

LLMs are fat-fingered geniuses. Logic engines are idiot savants.
Clam bridges them — in Perl.

Clam is an AI harness built over a SQLite blackboard and a
pub/sub bus inspired by Minsky's Society of Mind. LLMs handle
pattern recognition and intuition. Datalog, rules engines, FSMs,
and SAT solvers handle deliberate reasoning. The blackboard
coordinates everything.

Perl is the backbone because Larry Wall designed it to fit human
language instincts, and LLMs are trained on that text. The harness
can read, write, and extend its own infrastructure. That's the
thesis, and the working codebase proves it.


What's in the box
------------------

Core harness:
  - Agent loop (ported from Pi)
  - 4 tools: read, bash, edit, write
  - SQLite store + pub/sub bus
  - LLM providers: Ollama, OpenAI, Anthropic, Gemini, Azure

88 curated wits across 6 decks:

  logic   33 wits   Datalog engine, rules DSL, FSM, behavior trees
  git     10 wits   Git operations (status, log, diff, blame, ...)
  fs      18 wits   Filesystem operations (read, write, edit, ...)
  db       8 wits   Database shell (connect, query, execute, ...)
  perl    16 wits   Perl development (syntax, review, POD, tests)
  psh      3 wits   Perl Shell REPL (eval, vars, help)

30 more wits available: search, critic, SAT solver, OS primitives,
remote SSH. Install on demand.

  527 tests, all offline. prove -l t/ green.


Quick start
-----------

  # with LM Studio running locally:
  clam

  # with Ollama:
  CLAM_PROVIDER=ollama CLAM_MODEL=codellama clam

  # with OpenAI:
  CLAM_PROVIDER=openai CLAM_MODEL=gpt-4o clam

  # programmatic:
  perl -Ilib -e '
    use Clam::Driver;
    my $d = Clam::Driver->new(provider=>"ollama", model=>"codellama");
    $d->start;
    my $r = $d->ask("list the files in the current directory");
    print $r->{response};
    $d->close;
  '


Development setup
-----------------

  cd clam
  perl link_wits.pl        # symlink wits/ into lib/
  prove -l t/              # run core tests
  prove -l wits/*/t/       # run wit tests

link_wits.pl creates relative symlinks from lib/Clam/Wits/* to
each wit's lib/ directory. This makes the dev tree behave like an
installed CPAN tree — all modules load from -Ilib.

  perl link_wits.pl --clean   # remove symlinks

The symlinks are gitignored. Run link_wits.pl after clone.


The Perl thesis
---------------

Perl is not just the implementation language. It's the foundation
of a new kind of execution environment.

In a standard AI harness, the LLM generates code and the harness
runs it. In Clam, the LLM generates Perl, and Perl IS the
environment. The harness can modify itself. The LLM can extend
the harness by writing wits. Logic engines can collapse expensive
LLM calls into fast deterministic rules. The system gets cheaper
and faster the more it's used.

It's a Perl shell for the age of AI.


Questions for testers
---------------------

Clam is new and we're figuring things out. We need your help
thinking through these:

Wits vs CPAN — We built a custom plugin system (Wits) with
deck manifests, install-time testing, and a lockfile. But CPAN
already solves distribution, dependency resolution, and testing.
Should we replace Wits with CPAN? Or is a custom runtime layer
the right call, with CPAN as the distribution backend?

perlbrew — Should Clam manage Perl versions via perlbrew? A
harness that can switch between Perl 5.36 and 5.40 for different
projects has real value. But it adds a dependency. Is it worth it?

The UI — What does a great AI harness UI look like? We have a
REPL (Term::ReadLine) and an NDJSON daemon. Is that enough? Should
we build a TUI? A web interface? What would make you actually use
this daily?

Tools — We ship read, bash, edit, write (Pi parity) plus 88 wits.
What tools are missing? What would make Clam indispensable for your
workflow? Database migrations? Docker management? CPAN module
authoring? CPANTEST reporting?

The blackboard — Minsky's Society of Mind says intelligence
emerges from many simple agents communicating via a shared memory.
Our bus is the blackboard. The logic engines, the LLM, and the
user all publish and subscribe. Does this architecture hold up in
practice? Or is it over-engineered for what people actually need?

Crystallization — When the LLM solves a problem, can we capture
the solution as a deterministic Perl rule that runs faster and
cheaper forever? The logic deck makes this possible. Is it
actually useful? What would you crystallize?

The Perl hook — LLMs can read and write Perl natively. This means
Clam can modify its own wits, rewrite its own tools, and evolve
during a session. Is this a feature or a footgun? How do we make
it safe?

What can Clam unlock — We think a Perl AI environment enables
things no other harness can: self-modifying code, deterministic
reasoning over probabilistic generation, a complete unix shell
experience with AI assistance. What possibilities do you see that
we haven't thought of?


Get involved
------------

Clam is open source. The repo is at git@psycho:~/clam.git.

  git clone git@psycho:~/clam.git
  cd clam
  prove -l t/

Read docs/ROADMAP.md for the full architecture and vision.
Read docs/Wits.md for the wit system design.
Read docs/DRIVER.md for the programmatic API.

If you're a Perl developer who wants to build the future of
AI-assisted development, we want to hear from you.
